    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `ExceptionInfo.value`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `value` property in the `ExceptionInfo` class is designed to retrieve the exception value after an exception has been caught within a context manager. It exists to provide access to the actual exception object that was raised, allowing developers to inspect or handle it appropriately. The property does not take any parameters; it relies on the `_excinfo` attribute, which is a tuple containing information about the exception. The property returns the exception value, which is the second element of the `_excinfo` tuple. Before returning the value, it asserts that `_excinfo` is not `None`, ensuring that the property is only accessed after the context manager has exited and an exception has been caught. If `_excinfo` is `None`, it raises an `AssertionError` with a message indicating that `.value` can only be used after the context manager exits. This property does not have any side effects; it simply provides read-only access to the exception value.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
